00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.700
And how do you move that
into the modern world?

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.649
[INAUDIBLE] Rick and Kevin
are going to be talking about.

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:25.190
And so what we're
going to talk about

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:28.297
is the concept of
a data warehouse.

00:01:28.297 --> 00:01:29.630
It's a really novel idea, right?

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.400
We're going to
take all our data,

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.300
and we're going to
put it into one place,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.120
and then we're going to
correlate and analyze it,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.520
and we're to be data driven.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.300
Awesome.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.180
But the reality is that
data warehousing is really

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.650
difficult. It's challenging.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.820
The complexity piles
on as your business

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.910
grows, especially, right?

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:53.406
It gets really, really difficult
to start getting business value

00:01:53.406 --> 00:01:55.280
out of technology, so
much so that technology

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.901
can stand in the way
of your business, which

00:01:57.901 --> 00:01:59.400
is the last thing
you want it to do.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.520
Technology should be an
accelerator of your business.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.172
So it's really, with
the advent of Cloud,

00:02:06.172 --> 00:02:08.630
and cloud-native technologies,
and the scalability and high

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.669
level of manageability,
maybe, perhaps,

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:15.080
it's time to rethink what a
data warehouse really means.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.669
So BigQuery is the world's
only serverless data warehouse.

00:02:19.669 --> 00:02:21.210
Serverless, of
course, is a buzzword.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.376
But what do we mean by that?

00:02:22.376 --> 00:02:25.340
We mean that we entirely
abstract away hardware.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.840
We provide a very,
very high level

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.190
of manageability
automation, API first.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.630
We provide virtually unlimited
scalability, low effort,

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:41.700
low maintenance, to reach and
maintain peak performance,

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:47.270
and so on and so forth, having
the ability to share data

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.670
without moving the data
around, bringing people

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:53.650
to the data rather than
the other way around.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.740
And we really have evolved as
a service over the past six

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.450
years.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.880
These are some of
the things that we've

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.910
delivered as features
to our customers

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:06.800
over the past years
that really make

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.160
it easier for folks to run
a data warehouse on top

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.050
of BigQuery.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.420
Well, today, we announced
a number of really, really

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:15.920
interesting features.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.840
And I'll walk you guys
through some of these.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.256
Of course, you've heard about
declarative machine learning

00:03:20.256 --> 00:03:21.950
in BigQuery, which
brings machine

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.380
learning to the analyst.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.900
Folks don't need to know
TensorFlow, or Keras, or any

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.120
of these really, really
complex technologies.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.439
They can just use plain, old SQL
to prototype and train machine

00:03:34.439 --> 00:03:35.105
learning models.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:42.260
Let's talk about one of
those features-- clustering.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.000
In this particular
example, I have

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.080
a query that is selecting
a date and user ID.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.940
Well, clustering
increases data locality

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.070
for high-cardinality
fields so that if you

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.530
apply clustering
on your tables, you

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:58.820
get vast improvements in cost,
vast improvements in efficiency

00:03:58.820 --> 00:03:59.940
or performance.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.130
In some cases, the performance
can be 10 or 100 times

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.620
better than without clustering.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.750
And actually, at 3 o'clock,
Jordan Tigani and Lloyd Tabb

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.360
are going to be doing a live
demonstration of clustering.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.330
You should not miss it.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.149
We also announced
a new BigQuery UI.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.390
So it's material design.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:20.540
It's in the Cloud Console.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.120
It's localized to all the
different regions that

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.140
don't necessarily speak English,
with other great benefits--

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.270
for example, Data Studio
Explore, which allows you

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.340
to pop out your
results of your queries

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:37.327
into Data Studio for interactive
pivoting and further analysis.

00:04:37.327 --> 00:04:39.410
One feature that we haven't
really discussed much,

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.118
but we've had for a
while, and Home Depot

00:04:41.118 --> 00:04:44.000
will talk about it a little
bit in a little bit more detail

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.710
is hierarchical reservations.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:49.220
So for complex enterprises that
need isolation and resource

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.380
guarantees that would like
to have more control, more

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.400
knobs in their enterprise
data warehouse,

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.620
are able to create these
hierarchical reservation

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:02.210
trees that really
guarantee resources

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:03.500
for specific purposes.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.960
But also, the beauty
of this is that these

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.515
aren't silos of resources.

00:05:09.515 --> 00:05:12.760
If a data science project
is idle for some reason--

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.250
the data science team
went on a vacation,

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.370
and those resources are unused,
transparently, seamlessly,

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.276
down to subsecond
latency, those resources

00:05:21.276 --> 00:05:23.650
become available for the rest
of the organization to use.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:28.060
So as other teams continue
to scale on BigQuery,

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.710
the entire
organization benefits.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.830
You get economies of scale.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:34.780
And of course, one
very challenging aspect

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.880
of running a data
warehouse is ingest.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.960
We hear this from
customers all the time.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.440
How do I get data into
my data warehouse?

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.260
How do I get it so that this
data is fresh and available

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:48.010
right away, and it doesn't
compete with my query capacity?

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:49.920
And we've really
invested heavily

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.960
in our ingest capabilities.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.380
Our batch ingest is free.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:54.169
It's powerful.

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:55.960
We have customers
loading petabytes of data

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.210
into BigQuery every single
day without affecting

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.410
their query performance
even one bit.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.190
And of course, we
have a streaming API

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.690
on the other side,
which gives you access

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:08.350
to your data in the real time.

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:12.610
But enterprises
heavily rely not just

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:16.420
on native bespoke tools
provided by cloud vendors,

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:18.590
but on their partner ecosystem.

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:21.040
So if you're a large
organization that

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:23.949
have a history of
technology innovation,

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:25.990
you are probably using
lots of, lots of partners,

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:27.100
lots of vendors.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:32.320
And we will continue to invest
in our partner ecosystem.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.770
And finally, we have
lots of customers

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.560
from all kinds of scales,
all kinds of complexities,

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.170
all kinds of
industries and ranges.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:43.620
And they're all
eager to discuss,

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.040
to share their stories and their
journeys with you guys here.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.760
And potentially, hopefully,
next year, some of your logos

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.002
