00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.510
So to kick it off here, a
little bit about the Home Depot

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:14.560
analytics landscape.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:20.830
So we run our business on the
EDW, on our on-prem EDW today.

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.900
So we have hourly
sales reporting

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.820
that needs to go
out to the field.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.980
We have all of our
supply chain forecasts

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:29.860
for making sure all the right
products make it to the stores.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:31.660
We have all of our
marketing campaigns

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.450
that go out to
all our consumers.

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:36.420
That comes off the
data in the EDW.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.810
All our event reporting for
Black Friday, red, white,

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:41.830
and blue sales, all of
the store performance

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.710
scorecards for Monday morning,
reporting at the stores--

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.510
all of this is
coming off our EDW

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:51.520
and needs to be there on time
and be accurate every day.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:54.040
So we have very strict SLAs.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.219
And we have a wonderful
business community

00:09:56.219 --> 00:09:58.510
that lets us know whenever
we miss it and we don't have

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:00.680
our data there that's accurate.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:02.720
So that's a little
bit of our world.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:04.870
But it is very operational
and part of what

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:06.250
we do all day, every day.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:13.700
Our business community is also
very invested in this space.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.090
We have a very--

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:20.620
self-service is very encouraged
with our business teams.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.660
Our model is, we kind of
have a two-fold model.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:24.670
We have more of
our data consumers.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:28.580
And those are the folks
that are running the stores

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:29.830
and running the merchandising.

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:31.189
They're typically using Excel.

00:10:31.189 --> 00:10:32.980
They're using MicroStrategy
to get the data

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:35.295
they need to do their business.

00:10:35.295 --> 00:10:37.420
And then we have the data
analysts and data science

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:39.360
community.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.340
They're utilizing
Tableau, R, SAS, Python,

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:44.050
all the different
tools, to drive

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:48.310
more of the ad hoc solutions
on a regular basis.

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:51.670
But one thing that we realized
is that the demand was growing,

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:53.140
and it wasn't going to stop.

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:55.540
So we were having a hard
time managing all that

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.700
on our on-prem environment.

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:59.560
We were told our
on-prem environment

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.680
was one of the busiest that's
out there in the United States.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:04.480
And kind of proud
of that, but it

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.750
also presented some
additional problems.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:11.692
So that led us to a
decision point here.

00:11:11.692 --> 00:11:12.640
All right.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:14.950
That led us to a
decision point for where

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:16.280
we wanted to go going forward.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:20.230
So our on-prem solution
had served us very well.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:23.590
It helped us get to a
consolidated single EDW.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:24.960
We were finding a ton of value.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.970
But we were spending a lot of
time on capacity management.

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:30.760
And every time it came
to refresh the hardware,

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:34.720
there was a large cost outlay
that we had to work through.

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.370
And two to three
years prior to this,

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:39.400
we had to do a
capacity expansion.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.790
And that was about five to
six months worth of planning.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.240
It was a three-day outage.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.790
There was a lot of work
to make that all happen.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:48.490
And then we used up all that
capacity in about a year

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:49.130
to two years.

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:50.916
And that capacity was gone.

00:11:50.916 --> 00:11:53.290
So we were now to the point
we had to do another capacity

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:54.730
expansion with another--

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:57.190
with all of that
work ahead of us.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:00.010
And then on top of that, our
business teams are saying,

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:01.390
all the technology is expanding.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:03.670
How do we start doing
more and more complex

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.200
analytics in that space?

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:09.250
So that's what led us to having
to do something a little bit

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:14.500
different, and really, a
migration to modern analytics.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:18.430
So we started taking
a look at what

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:21.430
that would look like to move
to a modern analytics platform.

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:23.810
And we know we wanted a
world-class platform, let

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:27.645
us use managed Python notebooks,
get into the machine learning.

00:12:27.645 --> 00:12:29.770
We wanted to make sure our
analyst community stayed

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:31.030
very self-sufficient.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:31.930
And we wanted to
make sure we were

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:34.270
solving for a lot of the
challenges on the development

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:37.100
side as well.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.310
So those were our objectives.

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:42.250
So we did go through a
pretty in-depth analysis

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.530
of the different platforms
that are available.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:47.680
And we did POCs with
the-- you name them,

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:50.440
we did it with them,
for the most part.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.270
We also did a very detailed ROI.

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:54.070
So yes, cost was a
big piece of this.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:57.130
And we did do all our homework
there, from an ROI perspective,

00:12:57.130 --> 00:13:00.947
to make sure we understood all
the different cost differences.

00:13:00.947 --> 00:13:03.280
And we also spent a lot of
time collaborating across all

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.035
of the key stakeholders.

00:13:05.035 --> 00:13:06.910
Security was probably
one of the biggest ones

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:09.370
we worked with, along with
our infrastructure teams,

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:12.144
as well a lot of the different
business teams as well.

00:13:12.144 --> 00:13:13.810
And as a result of
all of that analysis,

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:17.050
obviously, that led us to
select the Google Cloud

00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:18.850
Platform, and
specifically BigQuery,

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:21.910
to help us with this journey.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:24.862
So I'm going to turn
it over to Kevin.

00:13:24.862 --> 00:13:26.320
And he's going to
walk through some

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:28.990
of the technology and
architecture decisions

00:13:28.990 --> 00:13:31.170
that we made.

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:32.170
KEVIN SCHOLZ: Excellent.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:32.669
Thank you.

00:13:32.669 --> 00:13:35.245
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:42.145
So as Rick said, this was a
once-in-a-lifetime opportunity.

00:13:42.145 --> 00:13:47.060
If you look at large enterprises
and legacy data warehouses,

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.480
these are large
investments, a lot of data,

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:53.470
a lot of people basically
relying on this.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:55.630
So if you're about to pick
up and change everything

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:58.239
that you've known and move
it, we basically said,

00:13:58.239 --> 00:13:59.530
let's start with our wish list.

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:00.830
What can we do?

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:03.520
So we put together this
wish list of items.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:05.000
And we categorized it for you.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:07.435
So what we decided
to do was, if we

00:14:07.435 --> 00:14:09.070
were going to go
through this effort,

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:10.060
we were going to go all in.

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:11.860
We weren't just going
to pick up some stuff

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:14.620
and move it, do some
[? IAS ?] stuff,

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:15.947
and try to move things in.

00:14:15.947 --> 00:14:17.530
We decided, if we're
going to do this,

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:19.270
we're going to
modernize everything.

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:22.390
So we right away said,
we don't want a port.

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:24.710
We knew this was a huge step.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:27.730
So if we're going to get
to a modern platform,

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:30.790
it required that level of
trust and that level of a leap

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:32.480
to get there.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.570
The next thing we did
was, as Rick said,

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:36.180
we run the business on this.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:37.180
This is every day.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:38.360
It's required.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:39.970
So we needed a date.

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:43.240
A lot of agile practices, you
try to work on iterations,

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:44.800
and you deliver when you can.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:45.790
But we have real dates.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:47.230
We have real timelines.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.300
And we don't want to have people
between systems for that long.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:52.140
So we chose a time.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:54.580
And we gave ourselves a
couple of years to get there.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:57.760
This is not a minor undertaking.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:14:59.590
But we knew, as part
of that, as well,

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:01.620
is we needed to be agile
in what we were doing.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:03.370
We were going to learn as we go.

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:05.860
We also were working
with the Google team

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:09.370
on features that weren't
available yet in BigQuery.

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:11.140
And we were pushing
in terms of things

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:14.620
that we needed to run our
business on the platform.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:16.630
So we knew part of that
would be to be agile.

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:19.590
And we would have to
change along the way.

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:22.060
When we look at the people
that were involved--

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.010
so we have not
only our analysts,

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.660
but our folks that
run the platform--

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:28.540
we said, what do we
want to do with them?

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:30.710
So if we're going to
make this big change,

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:33.190
we have to first
adopt new practices.

00:15:33.190 --> 00:15:37.000
So part of Home Depot's
general change has been,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:41.540
we want to adopt full-stack
teams, localized, very common.

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:43.210
But in a data
warehouse space, that's

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:45.045
kind of a novel or a newer idea.

00:15:45.045 --> 00:15:46.420
It's happening
more and more now.

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:50.090
But a couple of years ago,
it wasn't very prevalent.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.630
We also wanted to invest in
all the newest technologies, so

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:55.300
the cloud involved, as well
as all the things surrounding

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:56.380
that.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:58.986
And we wanted to make sure
that no one was left behind.

00:15:58.986 --> 00:16:01.360
So we wanted to have a learning
path for all of the folks

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.250
that knew maybe the
old set of tools

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:05.650
or the old set of
technologies to move over

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:10.010
to the new platform and have
the time to learn that platform.

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:14.210
When you look at the tech, we
made some big decisions here.

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:17.284
Our teams on prem that
run our data-- in our data

00:16:17.284 --> 00:16:18.700
centers, all of
our technologies--

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:20.030
they do a great job.

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:23.060
But that's a large investment
and a lot amount of time.

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:26.170
So we wanted to use as much
managed services as we could.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:28.480
So as Rick said, when we
evaluated different cloud

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:30.400
offerings, that
was a huge thing.

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:32.892
We didn't want to have all
the stress of the DevOps.

00:16:32.892 --> 00:16:34.600
It's hard enough to
make sure the data is

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.880
right, delivered on time,
and with all the changing

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:38.530
requirements.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:40.120
And the biggest
takeaway from that

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:42.460
is, we wanted to scale
up and down faster.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:44.860
Rick showed you the chart
of the CPU use, right?

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.200
If we wanted to grow
that very quickly,

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:49.090
that's a multi-month effort--

00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:50.722
large dollar times.

00:16:50.722 --> 00:16:52.930
With the cloud, you're able
to scale it much quicker.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:54.970
So that was a huge win for us.

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:57.220
One of the things we
also decided to do

00:16:57.220 --> 00:16:58.820
was around ETL tools.

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:01.630
So there's a lot of
great tools out there--

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:04.480
Informatica, Ab Initio--
pick your favorite one.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:06.579
We decided, as part
of this change,

00:17:06.579 --> 00:17:09.940
as well, that if we're going
to be modernizing the platform,

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:12.520
that we were going to modernize
the way that we brought

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:14.140
the data into the platform.

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:16.990
So we took a much more
developer-centric role.

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:20.829
So today, where we are, most of
the ETL and the data pipelines

00:17:20.829 --> 00:17:23.349
are co-driven, and
they're developer driven,

00:17:23.349 --> 00:17:25.240
and they're using modern tools.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:28.557
And we don't really focus on
some of those classic tools.

00:17:28.557 --> 00:17:29.890
There's nothing wrong with them.

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:31.330
It's just the
choice that we made.

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:35.940
So from this wish list,
we basically said, OK,

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:36.940
how can we achieve this?

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.280
So I'll fast forward the answer.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:40.810
Everything on here, we did.

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:45.400
So luckily, we had great support
from our leadership team.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:47.984
And it drove us
to an architecture

00:17:47.984 --> 00:17:49.150
that we'll walk you through.

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:50.860
So we're going to take a
little bit of time with this

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:53.320
and walk you through all
the different pieces of how

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:54.010
we did this.

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:57.130
So let's start with
the capture part.

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:59.540
So looking at this
from left to right--

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:02.080
so down at the bottom,
our OLTP sources.

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:04.720
OLTP is our operational
systems, where

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:08.290
you place orders, sales through
the front-end registers,

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:10.660
all of the interactions
with the company.

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:13.450
All of those are going to remain
in those operational systems.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:16.270
They may move over time,
but they are where they are.

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:19.990
Today, we have
thousands of databases,

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:23.230
file sets all over the place,
in prem, from customers,

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:25.930
from partners, as well
as streaming messages.

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:28.960
We have a lot of things that
are moving all the time.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:30.610
All these things
have dependencies.

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:31.870
And they're all scheduled.

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:34.210
And they have tight
timelines on them.

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:38.100
So our on-prem scheduling is
Tivoli Workload Scheduler.

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:39.900
So one of our
requirements is that we

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:41.760
have to wait-- when
a workload is done,

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.200
we have to be able
to pick that up

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.697
from a dependency point of view
and then be able to move it.

00:18:46.697 --> 00:18:48.780
For our main scheduling,
though, within the cloud,

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:50.970
we didn't want to
bring that product out.

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:53.490
So we use Jenkins as
a way-- and we script

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:57.840
that environment to basically
act as that bridge into the way

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.940
that we're moving.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:03.030
So here, you'll see Hadoop
with a fancy name data factory.

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:04.440
That's our name.

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:06.540
So one of the things
we wanted to do

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:08.569
with all the complexity
in the data center,

00:19:08.569 --> 00:19:10.860
with all these databases,
with all these queues and all

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:12.790
these files moving around--

00:19:12.790 --> 00:19:14.570
If you look at
that surface area,

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:19.350
there is a lot of systems, and
firewalls, and a lot of things

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:20.550
you have to jump through.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:24.210
So we didn't want to
expose all of that, and all

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:26.130
that complexity,
and all that work

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:28.620
each time a team needed to move
a different data source out

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:30.090
to the cloud.

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.380
We have invested in
Hadoop a long time ago.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:35.040
So we have a large on-prem
Hadoop infrastructure.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:38.520
So the teams knew how to
move data to that platform.

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:41.550
It was also very well
secured, isolated, segmented,

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:43.800
and it scaled
really well on prem.

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.680
So we made the choice of using
that platform as basically

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:48.860
saying, get the data here.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:50.800
And all the teams
knew how to do that.

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:53.280
It's relatively easy
within the company.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:54.780
If you can get it
there, we can then

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:57.340
create a factory or a
pipeline to move it out.

00:19:57.340 --> 00:19:58.620
It's bidirectional.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:00.840
So that, basically,
is hiding all

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:02.790
of our complexity
of our data center

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:05.190
and all the complexity
of those other systems.

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:08.700
I'd say, when we first
started, we had a lot of teams.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.880
We built, probably, four or
five different versions of this.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:13.620
But we've centralized
on one version,

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:16.174
because the more we can
have one way of doing it,

00:20:16.174 --> 00:20:18.090
it makes it easier for
all those dependencies.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:21.030
And we don't have teams
spending time on plumbing code.

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:23.740
It can basically
move a lot faster.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:26.340
So the capture side--
that's kind of the story.

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:29.470
We are making use of that
large infrastructure we have.

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:31.710
So our next part
is really, hey, how

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:33.600
do we get it out to the cloud?

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:38.370
So there's many ways companies
can peer with Google.

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:40.475
We use our own way.

00:20:40.475 --> 00:20:42.850
It doesn't really matter the
company we're going through.

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:44.670
But we have large,
10G connections

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.640
through multiple connection
points from our data centers

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:50.410
and our partners into Google.

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:52.020
The first thing we
do is, we basically

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:55.680
wanted to get the data to
the cloud, so a classic data

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:56.190
lake, right?

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:57.600
The buzz word around
getting it there.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.016
But for us, what
that really means

00:20:59.016 --> 00:21:01.890
is, source-similar data
from those systems,

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:04.230
in the format that
it was, typically

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:07.920
landed into a GCS storage
bucket or into BigQuery table

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.000
in its raw format.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.025
So that's source-similar,
minimal changes.

00:21:11.025 --> 00:21:12.480
So I'll give you
a sample change.

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:15.090
If it's coming out of an
older mainframe system--

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:17.400
the EBCDIC to ASCII
translations have been done,

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:18.870
any character set adjustments.

00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:20.700
Those things, we take care of.

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:22.770
But once it lands there,
it's not been modified.

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:26.590
It's basically as it was
from that source system.

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:28.432
So that's a
relatively easy step.

00:21:28.432 --> 00:21:29.640
We've got that on-prem piece.

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.610
We move it out.

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:33.560
Now, we're really into
the processing phase.

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:35.450
We've got it there.

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:41.160
So the next part is classic
ETL or ELT transformations

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:43.306
that you have to do
on those data sets,

00:21:43.306 --> 00:21:44.930
as well as we have
streaming data sets.

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:47.660
So we have a lot of
sources of sales,

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:49.760
of inventory, of
order stuff that's

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:53.310
basically moving out of those
source systems in real time.

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:55.160
So we wanted that to
flow into the system,

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:56.840
as well, in real time.

00:21:56.840 --> 00:21:58.340
So we have all these
different ways.

00:21:58.340 --> 00:22:02.150
Most of our processing that
we do today, it's either--

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:04.400
a lot of it is BigQuery
within SQL itself.

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:06.230
We use Dataflow.

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:10.220
We use some custom Java
running in App Engine.

00:22:10.220 --> 00:22:12.500
We have a wide variety of ways.

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:14.750
Again, with the full-stack
development teams,

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:17.150
they have some freedom to
choose amongst those platforms

00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:20.060
based on what's the right
use for their use case.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:21.780
So the processing
is much more fluid.

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:24.390
What we tell the teams is
basically, start with BigQuery.

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:25.790
If it works there, it's easy.

00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:27.180
It's very easy to do it in SQL.

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:28.554
There's a lot less
code to write.

00:22:28.554 --> 00:22:29.690
You can go a lot faster.

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:31.370
If you need something
more custom,

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:34.130
then basically head that
direction if you need to.

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:36.550
For streaming, what
we did is, on prem,

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:39.440
we use a lot of legacy
or older systems,

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:43.680
like WebSphere MQ or
ActiveMQ, different things.

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:47.030
We also have some Kafka, as
well, in the newer systems.

00:22:47.030 --> 00:22:49.190
We basically wrote
a part that runs

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:51.320
on our Hadoop platform
that moves that out

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:52.269
to Google Pub/Sub.

00:22:52.269 --> 00:22:53.810
So all of the
streaming stuff ends up

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:56.254
with all those payloads
into the Pub/Sub messages.

00:22:56.254 --> 00:22:58.670
So from there, that's where a
lot of the App Engine pieces

00:22:58.670 --> 00:22:59.720
pick those up.

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:02.360
And then they begin to
transfer them into BigQuery.

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:04.340
So that's how the
streaming works.

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:05.990
But then we had
two real big parts

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:08.480
of that that we
wanted to go into.

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.480
First was what we call
our building blocks.

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:13.910
So Home Depot-- it's
a complex enterprise.

00:23:13.910 --> 00:23:15.110
We have a lot of domains.

00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:19.610
So domains, orders, customers,
finance, supply chain,

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:22.590
merchandising-- there's
a lot of different areas.

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:25.490
So the building blocks for us
are those independent areas

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.260
of data that are arriving.

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:28.940
So there's a large
domain in a series

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:30.110
of subdomains under those.

00:23:30.110 --> 00:23:32.550
But they're landing
into those, basically,

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:35.580
nonintegrated,
domain-specific areas.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:37.820
But as they're landing,
they're landing in BigQuery.

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:39.950
And they're in optimized
performance structures

00:23:39.950 --> 00:23:42.419
that they're able to
read from very quickly.

00:23:42.419 --> 00:23:43.460
And they're able to join.

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:46.270
They're still able
to be used for any

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:47.900
of the downstream processing.

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:50.300
But they're nonintegrated
with other parts

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:52.170
of the different domains.

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:55.100
So the next phase of
that is, we really

00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:59.330
take what we call an
ADS, Analytical Data Set.

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:02.360
It's really working backwards
from your analyst or your user

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:04.070
in terms of what
different domains do

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:05.640
they want brought together.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:10.340
So think about it as either
a materialized view or a view

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:12.290
without actually
materializing in terms

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:13.974
of just a query
set that's driving

00:24:13.974 --> 00:24:15.140
against the series of those.

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:17.720
But most of the time,
it's a materialized view.

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:19.580
So we create another
set of tables.

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:21.800
We then bring all the
different building block

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:23.050
domains together.

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:24.470
We'll collapse dimensions.

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:27.260
We'll do things to make
it very performant.

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:29.030
We also use nested
structures here.

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:30.530
So we'll talk a
little bit about how

00:24:30.530 --> 00:24:33.690
we use nested structures
inside of BigQuery to do that.

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:36.700
But the idea here is these
ADSes, these Analytical Data

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:39.780
Sets, and these building
blocks are the direct things,

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:42.260
the direct data sources,
that our analysts are

00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:45.050
going to use to basically
query the data sets

00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:46.340
and drive the downstream.

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:48.200
So they have to be performant.

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:50.870
Almost all, if not all
of them, are basically

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:54.300
in BigQuery today, with
very few exceptions.

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:57.060
So the next part of
that is our use case.

00:24:57.060 --> 00:24:58.460
So how do our analysts use this?

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:01.160
So we've kind of got
two different camps.

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:03.350
We've got our typical
business analysts

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:06.980
that are using more structured
or reporting tools, standard BI

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:08.390
things, Tableau.

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:10.820
AtScale, if you're not
familiar, is an OLAP cube

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:12.710
that reads off of BigQuery.

00:25:12.710 --> 00:25:16.550
MicroStrategy, which is a more
classic reporting, and SSIS,

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:19.430
which is cubes using
Microsoft technology.

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:22.610
Our data scientists are
doing much more active, more

00:25:22.610 --> 00:25:25.570
newer things with ML, Datalabs.

00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:29.250
And we still have a lot of R and
SAS in the environment as well.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:31.790
So they're basically reading
out of these data sets.

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:32.600
They have requests.

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:34.670
And we want these
to be self serve.

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:37.280
So the Tableau teams--
once we get them out there,

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:40.230
we have Tableau running
at Google as well.

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:42.974
So they are able to get
instances running out there.

00:25:42.974 --> 00:25:44.390
They can connect
to the data sets.

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:46.850
They can then build
self-service reports.

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:48.830
If they need data
or other components,

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:51.230
they can look at the
different data sets,

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:52.610
pull from any of the values.

00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:54.052
They can read from them.

00:25:54.052 --> 00:25:55.760
And then if they don't
have the data set,

00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:58.251
they'll work with
our analyst teams.

00:25:58.251 --> 00:26:00.500
And we can help them get the
new data sets brought in.

00:26:03.710 --> 00:26:07.910
OK, so kind of a quick review
of what products do we use?

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:09.980
So we use GCS.

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:13.550
We use Pub/Sub heavily,
Dataflow, BigQuery.

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:15.950
We do use Datastore.

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:19.610
From our compute side, we
use App Engine, GCE, and GKE

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:23.180
as well, StackDriver
for monitoring.

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:25.370
Analytics, we're
using Datalab, which

00:26:25.370 --> 00:26:27.260
is one of Google's products.

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:30.650
And we're beta testing
Datahub, Dataprep, and BigQuery

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:32.400
UI, which was, I
think, announced today.

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:35.290
But we've been using
that for a while.

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:38.090
On the Home Depot side, we
have written our own tools

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:40.490
to basically supplement
some of these things

00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:43.620
that we made it easier for
our internal teams to use.

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:46.740
We call them the Data
Pipeline and Analytics Engine,

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:48.500
some SQL editing tools.

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:51.650
And we basically provided
a data catalog to be able--

00:26:51.650 --> 00:26:53.750
so that teams can find the data.

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:56.040
We have a large set of data.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:59.150
One of the things that
makes Home Depot complex

00:26:59.150 --> 00:27:03.357
is the wide number of
businesses we do under one roof.

00:27:03.357 --> 00:27:04.940
If you think of paint
and tool rental,

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:07.040
there are large,
complete businesses

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:10.145
under our roof, outside of just
the pick up the merchandise

00:27:10.145 --> 00:27:11.030
and go out the front.

00:27:11.030 --> 00:27:13.730
So those domains make
that really complicated.

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:16.190
So finding a lot of the data
and connecting it together

00:27:16.190 --> 00:27:18.470
can be a challenge
for the business.

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:19.850
One small callout here--

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:21.865
when we first started
looking at this,

00:27:21.865 --> 00:27:23.490
and we showed you in
the earlier slide,

00:27:23.490 --> 00:27:26.030
we did have a large
Hadoop platform.

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:27.859
We do not use Dataproc.

00:27:27.859 --> 00:27:29.900
We basically don't have
much of the Hadoop stuff.

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:31.950
We rely on BigQuery
for that use case.

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:34.699
So we didn't really bring a lot
of the Hadoop stuff forward.

00:27:34.699 --> 00:27:35.990
Nothing wrong with the product.

00:27:35.990 --> 00:27:38.030
It was just, hey, we
said we're doing a leap.

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:40.550
So we jumped onto BigQuery.

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:43.306
We do have some of
our data scientists

00:27:43.306 --> 00:27:45.180
in some of the teams
that do use the product.

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:46.429
There's nothing wrong with it.

00:27:46.429 --> 00:27:50.210
But our core EDW
doesn't use it a lot.

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:53.490
OK, so what else did we do from
an architecture point of view?

00:27:53.490 --> 00:27:56.900
So if you guys are familiar
with any of the EDW things,

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:58.972
we did do some changes here.

00:27:58.972 --> 00:28:00.930
So one thing I'll show
you, our slot hierarchy.

00:28:00.930 --> 00:28:03.770
Tino showed you how that worked.

00:28:03.770 --> 00:28:06.440
We take credit for
pushing a little bit

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:07.660
to get that out there.

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:10.412
And we'll show you our slot
map and what it looks like.

00:28:10.412 --> 00:28:11.870
So we'll show you
that in a second.

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:13.619
The advantage of that
is, what we've found

00:28:13.619 --> 00:28:16.100
is that it allows the
teams within the company

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:21.800
to have a fixed amount monthly
that they're willing to--

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:23.690
x number of slots
they can pay for.

00:28:23.690 --> 00:28:26.100
It can be dedicated to
them when they need it.

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.916
So if they need more,
they can buy more.

00:28:27.916 --> 00:28:30.290
And the best part with the
hierarchy is if, like he said,

00:28:30.290 --> 00:28:32.873
if they're not using it, other
parts of the company can share.

00:28:32.873 --> 00:28:35.420
So everybody who goes
into that platform

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:36.980
or into that
structure-- everybody

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:39.746
wins, because you'll at
least get what you paid for.

00:28:39.746 --> 00:28:41.120
And chances are,
you'll get more.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:44.720
And we use that excess capacity
amongst all the different teams

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:46.080
all the time.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:49.610
So it allows you to plan for
peak, but everybody wins.

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:51.470
Another part-- we
made a big decision.

00:28:51.470 --> 00:28:54.390
In our older system, we used
a lot of surrogate keys.

00:28:54.390 --> 00:28:57.321
So you're coming out of
the operational systems.

00:28:57.321 --> 00:28:59.070
There's nothing wrong
with surrogate keys.

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:02.270
But we were putting surrogate
keys on to every table.

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:04.670
And then we had translations
back to natural keys.

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:06.609
So we made a decision
when we moved over here

00:29:06.609 --> 00:29:08.150
that we were going
to have everything

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:09.542
in its natural key format.

00:29:09.542 --> 00:29:11.000
We have a couple
of structures that

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:12.958
still use surrogate keys,
when you're combining

00:29:12.958 --> 00:29:16.910
disparate domains, possibly from
different business units where

00:29:16.910 --> 00:29:18.650
the keys don't match very well.

00:29:18.650 --> 00:29:20.090
So they're not eliminated.

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:23.000
But we've basically reduced
them as a standard way

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:24.221
that we went in.

00:29:24.221 --> 00:29:25.970
And we'll talk a little
bit about nesting.

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:26.876
So we do use nesting.

00:29:26.876 --> 00:29:28.500
So if you're not
familiar, in BigQuery,

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:30.340
you can use a nested structure.

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:33.080
It allows you-- my favorite
example-- think of an order

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:34.420
with-- you bought three things.

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:37.580
Those three line items
can be all within one row

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:39.290
as a set of repeating values.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:40.970
And you can query it within SQL.

00:29:40.970 --> 00:29:45.470
And it's one row that allows
you to have more performance

00:29:45.470 --> 00:29:47.414
in terms of data elimination.

00:29:47.414 --> 00:29:48.830
And you can get
right to that row.

00:29:48.830 --> 00:29:51.415
We also use nesting
to, in some cases,

00:29:51.415 --> 00:29:53.660
in those analytical
data sets, to collapse

00:29:53.660 --> 00:29:56.440
some of the dimensions, or
some of our master data tables,

00:29:56.440 --> 00:29:56.940
into those.

00:29:56.940 --> 00:29:59.510
So there might be org
structure, or codes tables,

00:29:59.510 --> 00:30:01.850
or other things that can
be present in the data set.

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:03.410
So you don't have to do joins.

00:30:03.410 --> 00:30:04.610
So we use nested structures.

00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:07.320
And we collapse them
into that row as well.

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:09.380
The other thing we do is--

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:12.080
the last one is really,
Google projects and views.

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:14.860
So we use Google projects--

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:18.380
as a company, we have a
lot of Google projects.

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:20.000
And we use them
to our advantage.

00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:22.400
So a lot of our data
sets, as they come in,

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:25.340
we have data projects
broken up by domain.

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:28.220
That's where all of the ETL
pipelines, all of the ingest,

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:29.510
and all of that works.

00:30:29.510 --> 00:30:30.470
It's all contained.

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:31.550
It's managed by IT.

00:30:31.550 --> 00:30:33.650
It's delivered out to
those other systems.

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:36.414
But it's a place that
basically controls the data.

00:30:36.414 --> 00:30:38.330
And you saw, in some of
those building blocks,

00:30:38.330 --> 00:30:39.320
they land there.

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:40.266
Anyone can read them.

00:30:40.266 --> 00:30:41.390
So we grant access to them.

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:42.650
But it's all there.

00:30:42.650 --> 00:30:44.905
But it isolates and
separates the different teams

00:30:44.905 --> 00:30:46.280
so they don't step
on each other.

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:47.821
So we have about
nine of those areas.

00:30:47.821 --> 00:30:51.530
And we can add a new one if
we need as the company grows.

00:30:51.530 --> 00:30:52.980
We use views as well.

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:54.290
So BigQuery provides views.

00:30:54.290 --> 00:30:58.280
But we use projects as views to
allow us to control the access.

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:01.730
So we can put different
folks, in terms of IAM roles,

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:04.220
or things, into different
groups, into those projects.

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:05.530
And we can control the access.

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:08.180
There might be PII fields
that they can or can't see,

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:10.790
or other data sets that
we can use those projects.

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:14.600
So we make use of Google's--
just part of GCP projects

00:31:14.600 --> 00:31:17.720
and views, part of BigQuery,
extensively to basically

00:31:17.720 --> 00:31:21.350
monitor it, so we don't have
one big project with all

00:31:21.350 --> 00:31:22.370
of our data it it.

00:31:22.370 --> 00:31:23.180
It's just too much.

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:26.520
So we make use of that.

00:31:26.520 --> 00:31:29.260
So here's our hierarchy.

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:30.680
Its a little bit crazy.

00:31:30.680 --> 00:31:32.211
So I'll walk you through it.

00:31:32.211 --> 00:31:34.710
So the way it works, if you're
not familiar with hierarchy--

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:35.543
so there's two ways.

00:31:35.543 --> 00:31:38.316
In BigQuery, you can
do pay as you query.

00:31:38.316 --> 00:31:39.440
It's a pretty standard way.

00:31:39.440 --> 00:31:41.810
Or there's BigQuery
slot structure.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:44.771
With this, it's a fixed dollar
that you can have per month.

00:31:44.771 --> 00:31:47.020
And you can basically lay
out a series of hierarchies.

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:49.400
So what we've done is,
we have different parts

00:31:49.400 --> 00:31:52.380
of the companies in different--
they're all off of a root node.

00:31:52.380 --> 00:31:55.790
So the way hierarchies work
is, siblings share first.

00:31:55.790 --> 00:31:59.960
So anything that's across, if
there's extra slots available,

00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:01.440
it'll share to siblings first.

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:03.830
So if I go to the lowest part
of the tree on the bottom

00:32:03.830 --> 00:32:07.970
left, for example, those two
nodes in the bottom-left corner

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:08.710
will share first.

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:10.280
So if either one
of them need it,

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:12.680
they share amongst themselves
before it would go up.

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:15.050
And that works for
every part of the tree.

00:32:15.050 --> 00:32:17.030
So what we have is all
the different groups

00:32:17.030 --> 00:32:18.770
in the company, from
our dot-com teams,

00:32:18.770 --> 00:32:22.700
to our engineering teams,
to our security teams,

00:32:22.700 --> 00:32:24.930
that all have different
slot allocations--

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:27.980
everybody's off of a root
node, because everybody wins.

00:32:27.980 --> 00:32:28.880
There's no loss here.

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:30.380
You always get a
guaranteed minimum.

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:31.490
And there's a good
chance you're going

00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:32.900
to get more than you paid for.

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:34.420
And that's what we found.

00:32:34.420 --> 00:32:35.474
And it works really well.

00:32:35.474 --> 00:32:36.890
So if you're
familiar with Hadoop,

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:39.410
it's very much like
the capacity scheduler.

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:40.910
It works the same way.

00:32:40.910 --> 00:32:42.370
But it's really valuable for us.

00:32:42.370 --> 00:32:44.360
It also, with a
guaranteed minimum,

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:46.850
it allows you to
control your SLAs

00:32:46.850 --> 00:32:48.240
and gives you more fine grain.

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:50.150
So what we do then
is, we attach projects

00:32:50.150 --> 00:32:52.080
into those different buckets.

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:54.440
So like I said, Home Depot,
we have a lot of projects.

00:32:54.440 --> 00:32:56.589
And we can attach them at
different resource nodes.

00:32:56.589 --> 00:32:58.880
That resource node then grants
them the amount of slots

00:32:58.880 --> 00:33:00.230
that they need.

00:33:00.230 --> 00:33:02.000
And if a team needs
a different one,

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:05.134
and they want to buy-- let's say
we've got a general SLA bucket.

00:33:05.134 --> 00:33:06.800
That's where we put
all of our workloads

00:33:06.800 --> 00:33:08.480
that have guaranteed SLA.

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:11.510
If we have a team that has a
project that needs a tighter

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:15.290
SLA, and we decide we want
to break that down and buy

00:33:15.290 --> 00:33:17.700
a little bit more slots, we
could create another node,

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:22.350
move that project over, and
they can get that guarantee.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:26.010
So I think, when the Google team
told us, when they saw this,

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:27.120
they went, oh my.

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:28.930
This is complex,
but it works great.

00:33:28.930 --> 00:33:32.010
And a good story-- the
day we turned this on

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:34.530
was the best day
we had in the EDW,

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:36.150
because before that,
we had basically

00:33:36.150 --> 00:33:38.010
everybody sharing one big pool.

00:33:38.010 --> 00:33:40.920
And it was-- you couldn't
control the minimums.

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:43.134
So everyone was
competing with everyone.

00:33:43.134 --> 00:33:44.550
The day that we
turned this on, it

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:46.620
was the-- we had huge workloads.

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:48.660
Everybody's jobs went
through very cleanly.

00:33:48.660 --> 00:33:51.340
And it was painless.

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:54.090
So let's talk the
other big part of what

00:33:54.090 --> 00:33:56.010
anyone considering
this type of move

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:57.810
would hit just like we would.

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:01.530
So security is a huge
part of everything.

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:03.880
I'm not really going to walk
through the details of what

00:34:03.880 --> 00:34:04.380
we did.

00:34:04.380 --> 00:34:06.900
I'm going to give you our
strategy and a strategy

00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:10.030
that you could use as you're
considering this move as well.

00:34:10.030 --> 00:34:13.139
So almost every company is going
to have a good, extensive set

00:34:13.139 --> 00:34:17.010
of security guidelines that
drive based on your business.

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:19.980
What we did is, we looked
at the data classifications,

00:34:19.980 --> 00:34:21.179
the separation of duties.

00:34:21.179 --> 00:34:24.600
We looked at our DLP and
exfiltration requirements.

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:26.457
And we basically
developed a strategy

00:34:26.457 --> 00:34:29.040
for how we were going to move
to the cloud, what we were going

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:31.020
to move to the cloud,
and the mechanisms

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:33.179
by which we secure it.

00:34:33.179 --> 00:34:35.130
A big part of that
is, what we found

00:34:35.130 --> 00:34:36.850
in talking to a lot
of other retailers,

00:34:36.850 --> 00:34:38.710
a lot of other folks
considering this move,

00:34:38.710 --> 00:34:41.130
is a lot of the
IT teams that are

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:44.460
more legacy-based companies,
when they look at the cloud,

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:45.420
this is a scary thing.

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:47.159
All of a sudden, we're going
to have all this data together

00:34:47.159 --> 00:34:48.300
in a large area.

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:49.469
It's a big deal.

00:34:49.469 --> 00:34:52.800
So we built a partnership
with a security team.

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:56.670
And to tell you, having them
use the platform as well

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:58.410
is a huge plus.

00:34:58.410 --> 00:35:01.040
So it breaks down the barrier.

00:35:01.040 --> 00:35:03.540
They're able to do more stuff
in terms of security footprint

00:35:03.540 --> 00:35:05.940
using the same toolset
that we're using for this.

00:35:05.940 --> 00:35:08.010
And it basically
worked really well.

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:10.620
What we found also is,
the adoption rate went up.

00:35:10.620 --> 00:35:11.970
The fear went down.

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:13.750
The understanding
really went up.

00:35:13.750 --> 00:35:14.970
So the fear went down
is the wrong way.

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:17.760
It's really the understanding
of the way the technology works.

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:19.530
But the one thing
we did work through

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:21.690
is, on prem, we have
a lot of native tools

00:35:21.690 --> 00:35:23.320
that we use for security.

00:35:23.320 --> 00:35:25.890
So when you look
at the cloud, we're

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:28.560
basically looking at what
the new tools are, basically,

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:30.720
out at the cloud, and
how to adopt those.

00:35:30.720 --> 00:35:33.600
So there might be, you have
x tool on prem that you use,

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:36.709
but a tool at Google might
be equivalent or better.

00:35:36.709 --> 00:35:38.500
And we would look at
those different tools.

00:35:38.500 --> 00:35:40.459
So think about the
strategy that you

00:35:40.459 --> 00:35:42.750
would look at when you're
considering a move like this.

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:44.790
And work with your
security teams on that.

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:47.010
And our end of this was
really better adoption,

00:35:47.010 --> 00:35:49.380
so faster adoption.

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:52.771
But we still put a huge
focus and spent a lot of time

00:35:52.771 --> 00:35:53.770
with our security teams.

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:55.260
It's never out of focus.

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:59.690
It's just a better approach
to go towards that.

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:02.060
OK, so if I could
give you some stuff,

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:03.830
some ideas in terms
of how would you

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:06.420
start a migration
similar to this--

00:36:06.420 --> 00:36:09.320
so first, be agile, because what
you think you're going to do,

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:10.430
it's going to change.

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:11.840
We've changed multiple times.

00:36:11.840 --> 00:36:15.860
And being agile has proved
excellent in that case.

00:36:15.860 --> 00:36:16.950
Evaluate your tools.

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:19.497
You're probably using
existing tools today.

00:36:19.497 --> 00:36:20.330
Look at those tools.

00:36:20.330 --> 00:36:22.310
Bring the ones forward
that make sense.

00:36:22.310 --> 00:36:25.100
Maybe adopt something new.

00:36:25.100 --> 00:36:27.640
I think our single entry
point out of our data center

00:36:27.640 --> 00:36:28.850
was a huge plus for us.

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:31.100
It made it a lot simpler.

00:36:31.100 --> 00:36:32.130
Keep your schema.

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:34.730
So if you have an existing set
of schemas, bring them over.

00:36:34.730 --> 00:36:35.720
Try them in BigQuery.

00:36:35.720 --> 00:36:36.790
Don't change anything.

00:36:36.790 --> 00:36:38.060
See if it works.

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:39.890
Optimize if it doesn't.

00:36:39.890 --> 00:36:42.182
When we started, we kind
of took an inverted way.

00:36:42.182 --> 00:36:44.390
I would recommend you just
bring it over and move it.

00:36:44.390 --> 00:36:44.900
Try it.

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:47.000
If it doesn't work, then
worry about performance.

00:36:47.000 --> 00:36:49.910
There's lots of dials
and things you can move.

00:36:49.910 --> 00:36:51.770
We did not start by
copying the data.

00:36:51.770 --> 00:36:53.630
We actually tried to
go back from source.

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:56.150
Would recommend copying
from your source,

00:36:56.150 --> 00:36:57.710
from your existing system.

00:36:57.710 --> 00:36:58.760
Use managed services.

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:01.460
We love them, couldn't
recommend them more highly.

00:37:01.460 --> 00:37:03.920
And work closely with your
third-party companies.

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:07.700
So a lot of the tool sets may or
may not be ready for the cloud.

00:37:07.700 --> 00:37:09.560
So I'll bring up
Rick to bring you

00:37:09.560 --> 00:37:11.935
through lessons learned and
some of our performance wins.

00:37:11.935 --> 00:37:14.306
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:19.030 --> 00:37:21.450
RICK RAMAKER: Thanks, Kevin.

00:37:21.450 --> 00:37:23.790
Now, we'll go back to
some of the fun stuff.

00:37:23.790 --> 00:37:27.850
So what is the result of all of
this great work that Kevin just

00:37:27.850 --> 00:37:31.370
walked through and
the team went through?

00:37:31.370 --> 00:37:34.930
And I will say, in short,
it is game changing.

00:37:34.930 --> 00:37:37.619
And it is, and the--

00:37:37.619 --> 00:37:39.910
all the additional enhancements
that are coming in that

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:43.690
are making it even a
better decision for us.

00:37:43.690 --> 00:37:45.160
So you see some
of the performance

00:37:45.160 --> 00:37:46.120
wins we've seen here.

00:37:46.120 --> 00:37:47.911
Yeah, I stacked the
deck here a little bit.

00:37:47.911 --> 00:37:50.170
These are our three best
improvements that we have.

00:37:50.170 --> 00:37:53.050
Your mileage may vary a little
bit on your actual performance

00:37:53.050 --> 00:37:53.770
you see.

00:37:53.770 --> 00:37:54.730
But these are real.

00:37:54.730 --> 00:37:57.790
We were seeing things that
were running for 8, 9, 12

00:37:57.790 --> 00:37:58.960
hours that are now running--

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:01.060
that are now completing
in a matter of minutes.

00:38:01.060 --> 00:38:04.022
And we had a ton of workload
that we couldn't even

00:38:04.022 --> 00:38:05.980
run on our on-prem
environment, because we just

00:38:05.980 --> 00:38:07.780
didn't have the capacity there.

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:12.130
And that was a cool
experience, to be

00:38:12.130 --> 00:38:14.555
able to see that stuff get
completed and be able to run.

00:38:14.555 --> 00:38:16.930
We actually had a-- it even
created a little of a problem

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:18.400
for us, because a
lot of the teams

00:38:18.400 --> 00:38:20.896
were so enthralled with all
the new stuff they could go do,

00:38:20.896 --> 00:38:22.270
we had all kinds
of folks working

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:24.340
on new stuff on
the cloud, and we

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:25.840
had to get everybody--
don't forget,

00:38:25.840 --> 00:38:27.298
we have to migrate
all of our stuff

00:38:27.298 --> 00:38:29.450
off the existing platform
by the end of this year.

00:38:29.450 --> 00:38:32.070
So we got folks--

00:38:32.070 --> 00:38:34.150
it was a good problem to have.

00:38:34.150 --> 00:38:37.460
So that's where we are from
a performance perspective.

00:38:37.460 --> 00:38:39.560
From a capacity
management perspective,

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:42.370
it's also been very
game changing for us.

00:38:42.370 --> 00:38:45.040
I can tell you, we had an outage
in the middle of the night.

00:38:45.040 --> 00:38:47.685
We ran out of memory
on one of our VMs.

00:38:47.685 --> 00:38:49.060
In the old world,
that would have

00:38:49.060 --> 00:38:51.850
been a lot of work to figure
out how to get that fixed.

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:54.289
In the new world, it was a
matter of bringing down the VM,

00:38:54.289 --> 00:38:56.080
increasing the amount
of memory, popping it

00:38:56.080 --> 00:38:58.621
up back up-- all stuff you guys
have probably seen and heard.

00:38:58.621 --> 00:39:01.720
But for us, that was a
pretty cool experience.

00:39:01.720 --> 00:39:05.766
I'll also say, we have a
major deployment going.

00:39:05.766 --> 00:39:07.390
Last week, even-- it
was just last week

00:39:07.390 --> 00:39:09.473
or two weeks ago, and we
were running a little bit

00:39:09.473 --> 00:39:12.670
behind in getting all of
our historical data loaded

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:15.700
into the cloud for this release.

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:17.830
We worked with our Google team.

00:39:17.830 --> 00:39:21.190
They swung a bunch of slots
over to us in about eight hours

00:39:21.190 --> 00:39:23.200
notice-- so thank you for that--

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:27.550
and used those slots for us
to complete that migration

00:39:27.550 --> 00:39:28.320
on time.

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:30.736
And we had all that data loaded
in about a day and a half,

00:39:30.736 --> 00:39:32.690
versus how long we
were planning on that.

00:39:32.690 --> 00:39:36.220
So from a capacity
management perspective,

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:39.300
it's been very helpful for us.

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:42.630
And then, from a delivery
side, we are real, and live,

00:39:42.630 --> 00:39:46.590
and running on the cloud at
a number of key areas today.

00:39:46.590 --> 00:39:49.984
We have all of our pro
reporting-- so all of our--

00:39:49.984 --> 00:39:51.900
we have a sales force
out in the field working

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:55.140
with a lot of our major
contracting companies

00:39:55.140 --> 00:39:56.160
we work with.

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:58.170
All of their reporting
is available to them

00:39:58.170 --> 00:39:59.700
running on the cloud.

00:39:59.700 --> 00:40:02.970
All of our services business,
where if you want to have

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:05.450
someone do it for you
versus doing yourself,

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:08.712
and all the-- they're doing a
measure for you at your home--

00:40:08.712 --> 00:40:10.920
all of their information is
available to you on the--

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:12.890
to that team on the cloud.

00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:15.270
Our CEO Dashboard that
goes out every week,

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:17.320
with tons of metrics
about running

00:40:17.320 --> 00:40:19.320
our business-- all of
that is available and runs

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:21.660
on the cloud.

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:25.472
We have our sharing of data
with all of our vendors

00:40:25.472 --> 00:40:27.930
that sell into Home Depot, and
that we sell their products.

00:40:27.930 --> 00:40:31.050
All of their data, we pile
it out to 75 vendors today

00:40:31.050 --> 00:40:32.660
so we can collaborate
on the data.

00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:35.755
All of that is available
on the cloud as well.

00:40:35.755 --> 00:40:37.380
And we have all of
our clickstream data

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:41.550
from all of our websites, about
800 terabytes worth of data,

00:40:41.550 --> 00:40:42.420
of clickstream data.

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:44.140
All of that, also on the cloud.

00:40:44.140 --> 00:40:44.820
So this is real.

00:40:44.820 --> 00:40:45.569
This is happening.

00:40:45.569 --> 00:40:50.590
And we're using it every
day for what we're doing.

00:40:50.590 --> 00:40:55.890
Last thing I want to touch
on is some of our learnings.

00:40:55.890 --> 00:40:59.340
And there are many of them.

00:40:59.340 --> 00:41:02.500
We could probably have a whole
presentation on what not to do.

00:41:02.500 --> 00:41:06.150
But instead, we'll focus on
some of our key learnings.

00:41:06.150 --> 00:41:07.740
I would say, one
of our biggest ones

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:10.320
was just recognizing
the complexity

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:14.160
of the change management
of migrating to the cloud.

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:18.870
So before we started this, our
team, from an IT perspective,

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:21.380
was your traditional
data warehousing team.

00:41:21.380 --> 00:41:24.210
So we had a lot of ETL experts.

00:41:24.210 --> 00:41:26.760
We had a lot of PII experts.

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:29.070
We had a lot of SQL
experts that were

00:41:29.070 --> 00:41:32.650
all experts in the specific
tools that we were using.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:35.520
And they were some of
the best that we had.

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:37.260
And we had data modeling teams.

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:39.522
We had DBA teams.

00:41:39.522 --> 00:41:40.980
We had all the
different teams that

00:41:40.980 --> 00:41:44.430
were more of the typical
center of excellence models

00:41:44.430 --> 00:41:47.490
that many
organizations utilized.

00:41:47.490 --> 00:41:49.530
And when we decided
to move to the cloud,

00:41:49.530 --> 00:41:53.730
that changed a lot, and a lot
of it from a team perspective.

00:41:53.730 --> 00:41:56.870
So the team now-- and a lot of
the folks are here in the room.

00:41:56.870 --> 00:41:57.990
They're awesome.

00:41:57.990 --> 00:42:01.380
They're Java, they're
Python, they're SQL,

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:04.260
they're full stack,
they're SRE mindsets.

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:07.230
And that was a
major, major change

00:42:07.230 --> 00:42:12.490
to move that team from where
we were to where we are today.

00:42:12.490 --> 00:42:15.450
And my advice on that one
is, put the team first.

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:19.680
So this is really, really hard
for the team-- all these words,

00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:21.780
all these new technologies.

00:42:21.780 --> 00:42:24.750
Easy to say, really
hard to execute it.

00:42:24.750 --> 00:42:27.360
So we did 10% days,
gave people a chance

00:42:27.360 --> 00:42:29.510
to learn different
things on the platforms

00:42:29.510 --> 00:42:34.140
and take their time
to go and learn those.

00:42:34.140 --> 00:42:38.670
We did a ton of training for
them in many different areas.

00:42:38.670 --> 00:42:39.990
You're going to make mistakes.

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:40.750
That's OK.

00:42:40.750 --> 00:42:43.260
Learn from the mistakes
and keep moving.

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.510
And give the developers
the opportunity

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:46.941
to make their own choices.

00:42:46.941 --> 00:42:49.440
We didn't dictate, you must do
things this way and that way.

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:51.190
They tried a lot of
different things.

00:42:51.190 --> 00:42:53.400
And we landed in a
pretty good spot.

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:55.690
I will say, you need to
balance that sometimes, too.

00:42:55.690 --> 00:42:58.530
At one point, I think we had
eight data pipelines built.

00:42:58.530 --> 00:43:00.420
And we realized that
we probably don't

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:01.690
need eight data pipelines.

00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:05.590
So we worked through it and
got to a good spot on that.

00:43:05.590 --> 00:43:09.000
I'll also say, your
productivity velocity also

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:10.720
takes some time to develop.

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:12.700
You're not going to be
as productive as you

00:43:12.700 --> 00:43:14.930
were or want to be on day one.

00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:15.660
And that's OK.

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:17.670
So take your time
to get to the point

00:43:17.670 --> 00:43:21.960
where you have that
velocity set for that team.

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:24.120
And we didn't even
bother putting together

00:43:24.120 --> 00:43:28.679
a road map on our deliveries
until that velocity was set.

00:43:28.679 --> 00:43:30.720
But at that point, once
you have a good velocity,

00:43:30.720 --> 00:43:33.030
definitely do get
a roadmap in place.

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:36.120
That really flips the
switch from learning mode

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:37.680
into, truly, delivery mode.

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:40.220
And we did that about
the end of last year.

00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:44.460
And now we're really
executing against our roadmap.

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:47.850
Second thing I would
throw out from a learning

00:43:47.850 --> 00:43:51.810
is to realize that this
change is just as big on all

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:53.610
of your consumers of the data.

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:55.650
So we spent a lot of
time on the IT side,

00:43:55.650 --> 00:43:58.110
because we're the IT team,
and we did a lot of work

00:43:58.110 --> 00:43:59.350
to get us through this.

00:43:59.350 --> 00:44:01.890
But at some point, all
of our business partners

00:44:01.890 --> 00:44:04.080
also had equivalent
amount of change.

00:44:04.080 --> 00:44:06.600
And there's a number of those
folks in the audience with us

00:44:06.600 --> 00:44:07.660
here today.

00:44:07.660 --> 00:44:10.990
So we built out an
analytics enablement team.

00:44:10.990 --> 00:44:14.940
It's a small team, probably
about 3, 4, 5 people.

00:44:14.940 --> 00:44:17.040
But their full-time
job all day every day

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:20.430
was to help out that analyst
community with this migration

00:44:20.430 --> 00:44:22.450
as well.

00:44:22.450 --> 00:44:24.120
So they helped out
with making sure

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:26.040
we're aligned on the
naming standards,

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:28.590
and how many
projects do we need?

00:44:28.590 --> 00:44:32.190
And when do we hit the views
versus when do we hit tables?

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:34.830
What training is required?

00:44:34.830 --> 00:44:37.800
And just helping out with a lot
of the overall communication.

00:44:37.800 --> 00:44:39.150
We're a big company.

00:44:39.150 --> 00:44:41.142
It's hard to catch everybody.

00:44:41.142 --> 00:44:43.350
I'm sure most of you guys
are using a tool like Slack

00:44:43.350 --> 00:44:44.340
for your communication.

00:44:44.340 --> 00:44:48.120
That's been our number-one tool
for communicating to folks.

00:44:48.120 --> 00:44:50.055
And it also helps all
of the other people

00:44:50.055 --> 00:44:52.180
going through this journey
to help each other, too.

00:44:52.180 --> 00:44:54.970
So we don't have to be the ones
that answer all the questions.

00:44:54.970 --> 00:44:58.740
All of that's been a
huge, huge help for us.

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:01.050
And then find your early
adopters and communicate

00:45:01.050 --> 00:45:01.680
those wins.

00:45:01.680 --> 00:45:04.410
We've got certain folks
in the organization

00:45:04.410 --> 00:45:06.300
that were all in on this early.

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:11.080
And they were great at helping
move this journey forward.

00:45:11.080 --> 00:45:13.156
Last thing I'll call
out to you is just,

00:45:13.156 --> 00:45:14.780
the new Google features
that are coming

00:45:14.780 --> 00:45:16.460
are really game
changing as well--

00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:19.530
I mean, the
improvements in the--

00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:22.400
with clustering and partitioning
has been super helpful.

00:45:22.400 --> 00:45:25.474
The joint performance is
improving all the time.

00:45:25.474 --> 00:45:27.140
We had to flatten
some structures to get

00:45:27.140 --> 00:45:28.399
the performance early on.

00:45:28.399 --> 00:45:30.440
We have to do less and
less of that today because

00:45:30.440 --> 00:45:31.481
of the joint performance.

00:45:31.481 --> 00:45:35.210
So thanks, Tino,
for all of that,

00:45:35.210 --> 00:45:38.484
and so forth-- the slot
reservations and so forth.

00:45:38.484 --> 00:45:41.150
We're still partnering with them
on a little bit more visibility

00:45:41.150 --> 00:45:42.560
on what's happening
on the platform.

00:45:42.560 --> 00:45:43.910
That's probably the
next thing we're

00:45:43.910 --> 00:45:45.534
trying to get some
help with Google on.

00:45:45.534 --> 00:45:48.640
But for the most part,
we're getting what we need.

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:52.560
So in closing, as I said,
this technology works.

00:45:52.560 --> 00:45:54.930
It's really been game
changing for what

00:45:54.930 --> 00:45:56.610
we're doing in our environment.

00:45:56.610 --> 00:45:58.680
We have buy-in across
the organization.

00:45:58.680 --> 00:46:01.470
Everyone is all in on
getting us migrated

00:46:01.470 --> 00:46:04.382
by the end of this year,
end of fiscal year.

00:46:04.382 --> 00:46:06.340
Our partnership with
Google has been fantastic.

00:46:06.340 --> 00:46:07.589
We've learned a lot from them.

00:46:07.589 --> 00:46:09.550
Hopefully, they learned
a little bit from us.

00:46:09.550 --> 00:46:13.810
And I think we're set up for
analytics for years to come.

00:46:13.810 --> 00:46:15.811
So step one, get
your data in place.

00:46:15.811 --> 00:46:17.310
And then hopefully,
next year we can

00:46:17.310 --> 00:46:18.726
talk about some
really cool things

00:46:18.726 --> 00:46:21.510
we're doing on the ML side.

00:46:21.510 --> 00:46:24.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

